WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.542 --> 00:00:03.753
So you design something in illustrator,
and now you're

00:00:03.753 --> 00:00:06.548
looking to send it to the printer
to get it printed.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:09.092
But you may be wondering,
is it ready for print?

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:11.511
Is it set up properly? Will it look right?

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:14.806
Well, I'm here to help you out
and solve some of those problems.

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:20.103
Now, I'm going to walk you through
just like a simple, CMYK PDF.

00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:23.398
We'll go step by step
to make sure that it's set up for print.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:26.109
Now the first thing will be document
setup.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:29.195
So that includes bleed your color spaces
Cmyk.

00:00:29.446 --> 00:00:32.532
Then we'll go into the swatches
and layers.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:34.868
Just cleaning that up
titling it all properly.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:37.120
Then it'll be the fonts.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:37.370
Yeah.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.416
Typography fonts
that good stuff as well as images.

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:43.626
So converting them from RGB.

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:46.212
And I'll show you a few things
to keep your file size down.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.714
And then we'll export the whole thing.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:50.383
Okay. Let me show you how it's done.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:54.137
So here we are in illustrator
and we are starting with documents set up.

00:00:54.471 --> 00:00:58.016
It's the only place to start because
you have to create a new file anyway.

00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:00.810
But it's also a really important step.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.230
And it's crucial
to have your documents set up

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.317
properly from the beginning to help

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:10.528
that print process be a lot smoother,

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:14.491
especially in illustrator there.

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:17.535
If you're not clicking this print tab here
this print tab

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:22.624
you have a chance
of getting sort of a fake Cmyk.

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:26.044
And I'll show you what I mean
by that in a little bit.

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:29.714
But again, this is really important
print tab.

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:33.051
And we're going to go ahead
and choose our file size here.

00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:36.638
So eight and a half by 5.5in

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:40.600
is this postcard size
that I am going to be working with.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.894
And I'll show you we're going to be
getting that print ready.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.270
Please bear with me.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.191
My text boxes are a little hiding
my numbers because I scaled everything up

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:49.567
so you could see it in a screen recording,

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.820
but for whatever reason, it's doing this.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.946
So we talked about Cmyk.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.949
So Cmyk, when you're clicking
that print mode appear,

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:58.284
you are getting a true Cmyk color.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:02.664
You see, the options here CMYK
and 300 DPI is already selected

00:02:02.997 --> 00:02:07.502
300 PPI, which is pixels per inch
when you're in the screen mode.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:12.090
But when you're talking DPI dots per inch,
it's sort of the same scene,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.095
but 300 pixels per
inch in an inch on print.

00:02:17.762 --> 00:02:20.473
It's laying down 300 dots.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.768
Really nice close dots in that inch.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.729
When you're on screen, it's only 72.

00:02:26.729 --> 00:02:30.692
So if you're taking the square
and you have something that is 72

00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:34.487
resolution
and you're trying to size it to 300,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:40.076
you're expanding it a little past
what it's made to do.

00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:44.080
And that's why a lot of times
you'll get fuzzy images.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.500
If you're trying to print a 72 dpi to 300.

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.962
So that's why
we it's really important to do 300

00:02:50.962 --> 00:02:53.965
because that is what printers output.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.303
So that’s Cmyk
that's the DPI and then bleed.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:02.432
So bleed is the it's an eighth of an inch

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:08.271
or 0.125in all the way around your design.

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:11.274
It's an extra outside
of your finished size

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:15.612
so that when you have full bleed,
background colors

00:03:15.737 --> 00:03:19.240
or imagery or something like that,
anything that's going to go

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.703
and be closing touch or past that cut size
that you want it to be,

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:27.415
that's what bleed is for
when you're printing something.

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:29.250
Print is a manufacturing process.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.502
So it's naturally
going to have these micro shifts

00:03:31.502 --> 00:03:33.838
when it's hitting the feed
and it's doing the things.

00:03:33.838 --> 00:03:36.925
And when you go to trim
that in a bulk stack,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.470
if you're trimming it
and you don't have bleed,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.307
you could potentially get these tiny light
slivers,

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:48.102
these white slivers of the paper
because your ink didn't go past right.

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:51.105
And it shifted. So bleed is crucial.

00:03:51.272 --> 00:03:56.569
And that is 0.125in all the way around.

00:03:57.362 --> 00:03:58.696
So we are good to go here.

